.TH RM 1 
.SH NAME
rm, rmdir  \- remove (unlink) files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rm
[
.B \-fri
] file ...
.PP
.B rmdir
dir ...
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Rm
removes the entries for one or more
files
from a directory.
If an entry was the last link to the file, the file
is destroyed.
Removal of a file requires write permission in its directory,
but neither read nor write permission on the file itself.
.PP
If a file has no write permission
and the standard input is a terminal,
its permissions are printed and a line is read from
the standard input.
If that line begins with `y' the file is deleted,
otherwise the file remains.
No questions are asked
when the
.B \-f
(force) option is given.
.PP
.B Note:
nor when the standard input is
.B not
a terminal.
.I Rm
sets
.B \-f
for itself in that case \- there being nobody to answer \- which v7's page never
said, and which is what makes
.I rm
usable from a shell script.
.PP
If a designated file is a directory,
an error comment is printed unless the optional
argument
.B \-r
has been used.
In that case,
.I rm
recursively deletes the
entire contents of the specified directory,
and the directory itself.
.PP
.B Note:
`and the directory itself' is done by
.B forking and execing /bin/rmdir,
which is where the privilege for it comes from:
.IR unlink (2)
of a directory is refused to anyone but the super\-user, and
.I /bin/rmdir
is set\-user\-id root (see below).
.I Rm
is
.B not
setuid and does not need to be \- but
.B rm \-r will not work if /bin/rmdir is missing,
and will say so.  (v7 tried
.I /usr/bin/rmdir
as well; there is no
.I /usr/bin
on this system, and that second attempt is kept and is dead.)
.PP
.B Note:
.B rm \-r
holds one file descriptor open per level of nesting, and
.B NOFILE
is 20 (include/sys/param.h), so it stops at twenty levels deep.  This was as true
of v7 as it is here.  The recursion also builds each path in a 100\-character
buffer, and refuses a name that will not fit \- v7 built it with no bound at all,
which mattered rather more in a routine that recurses on what it builds.
.PP
If the
.B \-i
(interactive) option is in effect,
.I rm
asks whether to delete each file,
and, under
.BR \-r ,
whether to examine each directory.
.PP
.I Rmdir
removes entries for the named directories,
which must be empty.
.PP
.B Note:
.I rmdir
is
.B set\-user\-id to root
on this system, for
.IR mkdir (1)'s
reason turned round: there is no
.I rmdir
system call, the directory is taken apart with three
.IR unlink (2)s
\- of
.RB ` name/.. ',
.RB ` name/. '
and
.I name
itself \- and
.I unlink
of a directory is refused to anyone but the super\-user.
The bit is granted in
.I root.manifest
at the top of the source tree, as mode 04755.  As in
.IR mkdir ,
the permission that actually decides is the program's own
.IR access (2)
check on the parent, made against the
.B real
user id.
.PP
.B Note:
.I rmdir
refuses to remove a name whose last component is
.B .
or
.BR .. ,
and refuses to remove the directory the process is standing in.  v7's
.I rmdir
made the first check on the whole path rather than on the last component, so it
was not made at all for a qualified name such as `rmdir foo/.'; that is repaired
here.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
unlink(2),
mkdir(1),
cp(1),
mv(1),
ln(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Generally self-explanatory.
It is forbidden to remove the file `..' merely to avoid the
antisocial consequences of inadvertently doing something like
`rm \-r .*'.
.PP
.B Note:
.I rm
exits with the number of files it failed on, and this now includes a directory
that
.I /bin/rmdir
failed to remove.  In v7 it did not:
.I rm
added the child's raw
.IR wait (2)
status to its error count, and a normal exit stores its code in the
.B high
byte, so a failed
.I rmdir
contributed 0400 and
.I rm
exited 0 \- reporting success for a directory that is still there.
.PP
.B Note:
.IR rm 's
own messages go to the standard
.B output
and not to the standard error, which is v7's and is inconsistent with
.IR cp (1)
and
.IR mv (1);
only `cannot remove `..'' goes to the standard error.  They are flushed before
each question is read, which they were not \- the standard output is line
buffered to a terminal on this system, so under
.B \-i
the question used to sit in the buffer while
.I rm
waited for an answer to something the user had not been shown.
